Draw the **Lattice inserter**, a 36-frame **sprite sheet** of an animated
swing-arm machine in three upgrade tiers, using only the drawing tool.

The inserter is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a top-down factory simulation
drawn **flat** — you are looking *down* on the machine, consistent with the
assembler, **not** drawing a side elevation and with **no faux-3D height**. You
are drawing one inserter mounted on the centre tile, viewed in its canonical
orientation: it **takes hold of an item on the tile to its left**, swings in an
arc **across the floor**, and **releases it onto the tile to its right**. You draw
it at **three tiers**, each a twelve-frame swing cycle:

- **Tier 1 — frames 0–11:** the base machine, with an **amber** arm and hand.
- **Tier 2 — frames 12–23:** a reinforced, faster machine with a **red-orange** arm
  and more mechanical detail.
- **Tier 3 — frames 24–35:** the most advanced, fastest machine with a **blue-cyan**
  arm, the densest detail, and a subtle energy glow.

Across each tier's twelve frames the arm sweeps from left to right holding (the
first six frames), then swings back empty (the last six), so the cycle loops
seamlessly. Draw the arm and hand only — **no item** — but **leave a clear slot at
the hand** for the renderer to draw the cargo into; the design of the gripping hand
is yours, as long as holding versus empty reads from the hand itself and the slot
stays clear. The pivot base sits at the centre of the frame; the arm sweeps across
the ground plane, not up the screen. The renderer rotates this one sprite for the
other facings, so you draw this one orientation only. The three tiers share one
grey-blue base, reach, and item slot, differing only in arm accent color,
mechanical detail, and playback speed, so they read as one inserter progressively
upgraded. The brief's **The style** and **The item slot** sections are the things
to get right.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a frame
with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a per-frame
action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 64×64 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 24 --y 26 --width 16 --height 12 --color "#4d5a64"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the inserter sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a fixed grey-blue base with a swing arm and
gripping hand in the tier's accent color, the hand holding as it sweeps an arc from
the left pickup to the right drop over the first six frames of each tier's cycle
and empty as it swings back over the last six — no item drawn, but a clear slot
left at the hand for the renderer's cargo — so each tier's loop reads as one
continuous grip-swing-release-return cycle. The three tiers share one base, reach,
and item slot, differing only in arm accent color (amber, red-orange, blue-cyan),
mechanical detail, and playback speed, so they read as one inserter progressively
upgraded. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your
finished submission.
